<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>

<section xml:id="sec_conceptual_check_local_minima" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Conceptual Check</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>A differential drive robot using potential fields enters a symmetric U-shaped obstacle region. The goal lies behind the back wall. The robot reaches the center of the pocket and stops before reaching the goal.</p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise xml:id="ex_conceptual_check_local_minima">
    <statement>
      <p>What is the structural root cause of the failure?</p>
    </statement>
    <choices>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>A catastrophic kinematics inversion error.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>Kinematics inversion errors are a low-level motion-model problem; they would not produce a stable stop at the geometric center of a symmetric pocket.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>A local minimum where attractive and repulsive forces cancel.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>Correct. This is a local-minimum trap: the goal attraction is exactly countered by the combined obstacle repulsion, creating an equilibrium point away from the goal.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Open-loop drift in the ultrasonic sensor array.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>Sensor drift would cause gradually incorrect readings over time, not a stable stop exactly at the center of a symmetric obstacle pocket.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>Bug 2 m-line parameters set to zero.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>This scenario describes a potential-fields controller, not the Bug 2 algorithm, so m-line parameters are not involved.</p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>
</section>
